{{also|Hinterland}}
==English==
{{was wotd|2015|October|5}}

===Etymology===
{{borrowing|de|Hinterland|lang=en}}, from {{compound|hinter|Land|t1=behind|t2=land|lang=de|nocat=1}}, cognate to English {{term|hind||back, rear|lang=en}} + {{term|land|lang=en}}. First used in English in 1888 by {{w|George Chisholm}} in his work ''Handbook of Commercial Geography'' originally as {{term|hinderland|lang=en}}, but the current spelling (following German) became more popular.<ref>{{cite web|title=hinterland|url=http://global.britannica.com/topic/hinterland|work={{w|Encyclopædia Britannica}}|accessdate=8 September 2015}}</ref> The term is characteristic of a [[thalassocracy|thalassocratic]] analysis of space (from the point of view of a nation, such as 19th-century Britain, with maritime supremacy).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈhɪntə(ɹ)ˌlænd/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# The [[land]] immediately next to, and [[inland]] from, a [[coast]].
# The [[rural]] [[territory]] surrounding an [[urban]] area, especially a [[port]].
# A [[remote]] or [[undeveloped]] area, a [[backwater]].
# {{context|figuratively|lang=en}} That which is [[unknown]] or [[unexplored]] about someone.
# {{context|figuratively|lang=en}} Anything [[vague]] or [[ill-defined]], ''especially'' one that is ill understood.
#* '''2007''', Lesley Jeffries, ''[http://www.palgrave.com/products/title.aspx?PID=262132 Textual Construction of the Female Body]'', abstract
#*: This approach utilizes concepts such as naming, describing, contrasting and equating to access the '''hinterland''' between structure and meaning, and to map out the subtle ways in which texts can naturalise the ideology of the perfect female form.

